  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nA shortwave currently nearing the Pacific Northwest coast will\ntrack across the Rockies and bring low to medium chances for\nrain, spreading west to east on Tuesday.\n\nDry and mild tonight with increasing high and mid level\nclouds. Overnight lows are forecast to be mainly in the 30s, but\na few upper 20s are possible. On Tuesday, the weak wave tracking\nthrough the area could produce up to a tenth of an inch or so \nof liquid precip, but think most will see little if any \nprecipitation as the lower levels of the atmosphere remain quite\ndry. The system is trending a bit slower than yesterday, which \nhas a couple of implications for our forecast on Tuesday. First,\nwith the slower arrival, temperatures will be warm enough for \nall rain by the time any precipitation arrives. Second, with the\nsurface low lifting into west central ND by around 18 UTC \nTuesday, warmer air should be able to sneak into southwest and \nsouth central ND, keeping temperatures a bit warmer than \nforecast. Will use an equal blend of the NBM/NBM90 for these \nareas. A little light rain may linger into Tuesday evening over \nthe south central into eastern ND, but for the most part, dry \nconditions are expected Tuesday night. \n\nA digging eastern U.S. trough will allow a shot of colder\nCanadian air to drop south through eastern half of North Dakota\nfor the Wednesday through Friday timeframe. This brings quite a\nrange of temperatures from cooler highs in the mid 30s to lower\n40s east to mild readings in the mid to upper 50s west (around \na 20 degree difference each day). As western ridging becomes \nmore established over the state by the weekend, the warmer \ntemperatures will spread east to include the entire forecast \narea. Highs on Sunday are forecast to be mainly in the 50s \nacross all of western and central North Dakota. As we head into\nnext week, we see the formation of a western U.S. trough, \nwhich, with downstream ridging should keep us mild through the \nearly portion of the work week, but possibly followed by a more \nactive pattern by mid-week as the trough decides how it's going \nto approach the Plains.",
